---
import Layout from '../../studio/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { mailto, studio } from '../../apps/registry';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — PingDeck';
const description = 'Privacy policy for PingDeck (com.yanerapps.pingdeck), an Android app by Yaner Apps that pings many hosts at once.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="prose">
    <div class="prose-header">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">PingDeck for Android (<code>com.yanerapps.pingdeck</code>)</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>Effective: from PingDeck&rsquo;s release on Google Play</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Updated: 23 Oct 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      This policy explains what PingDeck does with your data. PingDeck is made by Yaner Apps, an independent
      developer studio ("we", "us"). It applies to the PingDeck Android application and to this website.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <p><strong>In short:</strong> PingDeck has no user accounts and no servers of our own. Your host list,
      settings and ping statistics stay on your device. PingDeck sends probes only to the hosts you add, and
      requests only to the webhook and links you set up. The free version shows ads from Google AdMob; if you
      buy Pro, Google Play processes the purchase and payment, and we never see your payment details. We never
      sell your personal information.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Data that stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The following is stored in the app's private storage and is never transmitted to us &mdash; we operate
      no server and have no way to read it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your host list: addresses, host names, URLs, ports, descriptions and groups</li>
      <li>App settings, including your custom URL commands and webhook address</li>
      <li>Ping statistics and history. History is kept in memory while the app runs and is not saved to storage</li>
      <li>Your Pro status and, if you watched a rewarded ad, when your Pro trial ends</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can delete hosts, reset statistics, or clear all hosts and data inside the app at any time.
      Uninstalling PingDeck removes everything.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Network traffic you ask for</h2>
    <p>
      Monitoring means sending network traffic. PingDeck sends it only to destinations you choose:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>Who receives it</th>
            <th>What they see</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Monitoring (ICMP ping, TCP connect, HTTP and HTTPS checks)</td>
            <td>Each host you add</td>
            <td>Your IP address and the probe itself. HTTP(S) checks send a HEAD (or GET) request with Android's standard user agent, which names your Android version and device model</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Name lookups, including the optional reverse DNS lookup</td>
            <td>The DNS servers your device or network is set to use</td>
            <td>The host names and IP addresses being looked up</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Webhook (Pro, off until you enter an address)</td>
            <td>The web address you enter</td>
            <td>A request when a host goes down or comes back up, carrying the host details your template includes (such as host name, IP address, description, group, status and counts)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Custom URL commands and "Open in browser"</td>
            <td>The app that opens the link, usually your browser, and the site it visits</td>
            <td>The link built from your template and the selected host's details, only when you tap the command</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Local network discovery</td>
            <td>Devices on the local network you choose to scan</td>
            <td>ICMP and TCP probes, SSDP and mDNS discovery messages, and NetBIOS and reverse DNS name queries from your device</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Nothing here goes to us. Local network scan results stay on your device: PingDeck does not identify
      devices by MAC address or model and does not upload what it finds. HTTP checks to <code>http://</code>
      addresses and webhooks to <code>http://</code> addresses are not encrypted, because you chose a plain
      HTTP address.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Data sent to third parties</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Recipient</th>
            <th>What is sent</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Google AdMob and the Google User Messaging Platform (free version only)</td>
            <td>Your advertising ID (AAID) and app set ID, your IP address (from which Google may estimate a general location), device and app information, ad interactions, SDK diagnostics, and your consent choices</td>
            <td>To request and display banner and rewarded ads, and to record your consent</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Play Billing</td>
            <td>Your purchase of Pro and a one-way hashed identifier derived on your device</td>
            <td>To sell Pro, confirm that you own it, and help Google detect fraud</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Your host list, statistics and settings are never sent to Google or to any advertising service.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Advertising</h2>
    <p>The free version of PingDeck shows ads supplied by Google AdMob:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Banner ads:</strong> One banner at the bottom of the screen. It is not shown in Settings, while the keyboard is open, or in very short windows, and it never covers app content.</li>
      <li><strong>Rewarded ads:</strong> Never forced on you. One plays only after you tap a button offering four hours of Pro in exchange for watching it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      PingDeck shows no interstitial ads and no ads on a timer. Starting or stopping monitoring never
      requires an ad, ads never appear in the notification or the home screen widget, and monitoring keeps
      running while an ad plays. Once you buy Pro, PingDeck shows no ads and does not start the ad SDK.
    </p>
    <p>
      We use Google AdMob only, with no other ad network or mediation layer, and we have not added any
      analytics or crash-reporting SDK. The Google SDKs collect their own diagnostics, which we do not
      receive.
    </p>

    <h3>Consent (EEA, UK, and Switzerland)</h3>
    <p>
      If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, or Switzerland, PingDeck asks for your
      consent before ads are requested, using Google's certified User Messaging Platform. You can review or
      change your choice at any time under <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy options</strong>. If you decline,
      the app remains fully usable; Google may still show limited, non-personalised ads, and the rewarded
      trial is not offered while ads cannot be requested.
    </p>

    <h3>US state privacy laws</h3>
    <p>
      We do not sell your personal information, and we do not share it for cross-context behavioural
      advertising in exchange for money. Residents of US states with applicable privacy laws are shown a
      notice with an opt-out choice, delivered through the same Privacy options screen.
    </p>

    <h3>Controlling the advertising ID</h3>
    <p>
      Android lets you reset or delete your advertising ID at any time under
      <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Ads</strong> (the exact path varies by device). Deleting it stops
      personalised advertising system-wide.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google's handling of ad data is governed by the
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Privacy Policy</a>; see also
      <a href="https://support.google.com/admob/answer/6128543" rel="noopener" target="_blank">how Google uses data in AdMob</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Buying Pro</h2>
    <p>
      PingDeck Pro is a one-time purchase sold and billed entirely by Google Play. Google Play takes the
      payment, and your payment method and billing details stay with Google. We never receive your card
      number, billing address or other payment information, and PingDeck has no server that receives your
      purchases.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you start a purchase, PingDeck tells Google Play which product you chose, along with an identifier
      the app derives on your device with a one-way hash, which Google uses to help detect fraud. Google Play
      gives us order records for our sales in the Play Console, such as the order number, product, price and
      date, which we use only for accounting, refunds and support.
    </p>
    <p>
      To unlock Pro, the app asks Google Play whether you own it and keeps a signed copy of the answer (the
      Google Play purchase token and when it was last confirmed) in private storage, so Pro keeps working for
      up to 30 days when you are offline. This copy, and the end time of a rewarded Pro trial, are excluded
      from Android cloud backup and device-to-device transfer. After a reinstall or on a new device, the app
      checks with Google Play again; you can also tap <strong>Restore purchase</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Refunds and changes of payment method are handled by Google Play under your Google account. Google's
      handling of payment data is governed by the
      <a href="https://payments.google.com/payments/apis-secure/u/0/get_legal_document?ldo=0&amp;ldt=privacynotice" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Payments Privacy Notice</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Files you export</h2>
    <p>
      PingDeck can write files to places you choose: an exported host list, exported statistics, and with
      Pro an automatic export folder and a ping log. These files contain host names, addresses and
      measurements in plain text. They are created only when you ask for them, they are not uploaded
      anywhere, and once they leave the app they are yours to protect. Sharing an export through Android's
      share sheet sends it to the app you pick.
    </p>
    <p>
      Android's own cloud backup may include your host list and settings if you have enabled it on your
      device. That backup is managed by Google under your Google account, not by us.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Notifications and the widget</h2>
    <p>
      While monitoring runs, PingDeck shows an ongoing notification, and alerts when hosts go down or come
      back. These, and the home screen widget, can show host names and how many hosts are reachable, so
      anyone who can see your screen can see them. You can hide sensitive notification content on the lock
      screen in Android's notification settings.
    </p>

    <h2>8. What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No user accounts, sign-in, or profiles</li>
      <li>No servers operated by us, and therefore no copy of your data held by us</li>
      <li>No analytics or crash-reporting SDKs added by us</li>
      <li>No location permission and no use of your location</li>
      <li>No sale of personal information</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. Children</h2>
    <p>
      PingDeck is intended for adults. It is not directed to children, and we do not knowingly collect data
      from children. Its Google Play target audience is set to adults only.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Data retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Because your data lives on your device, you control its lifetime. Ping history disappears when the
      app's process ends; your host list and settings stay until you delete them or uninstall the app.
      There is nothing for us to delete on our side, since we hold nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Data held by Google as a result of the requests described in section 3, and anything the hosts, DNS
      servers or webhook you chose receive, is subject to that recipient's own retention practices.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Local data is kept in the app's private storage, which other apps cannot read on a non-rooted device.
      The ad and billing SDKs talk to Google over HTTPS. Traffic to the hosts and webhook you choose is
      encrypted only when you use an <code>https://</code> address; ICMP and TCP probes carry no content.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Additional information for the EEA, UK and Switzerland</h2>
    <p>
      This section gives the information required by the EU and UK General Data Protection Regulation and
      the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection.
    </p>

    <h3>Controller</h3>
    <p>
      The controller for the processing described in this policy is Yaner Yang, publishing as Yaner Apps,
      reachable at <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a>. Google acts as an independent
      controller for the data it receives, under its own policies.
    </p>

    <h3>Purposes and legal bases</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Data</th>
            <th>Legal basis</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Providing Pro you bought</td>
            <td>Your Google Play purchase status and purchase token, the hashed identifier sent with a purchase, and the order records Google Play provides</td>
            <td>Necessary to perform the contract you entered into when buying (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Personalised ads, and storing or reading ad identifiers on your device</td>
            <td>Advertising ID, app set ID, IP address, ad interactions</td>
            <td>Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a)), which you can withdraw at any time in Privacy options</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Non-personalised ads, ad measurement and fraud prevention</td>
            <td>IP address, device and app information, ad interactions</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in funding a free app and preventing ad fraud (Art. 6(1)(f)), and your consent where the law requires it for storage on your device</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Answering your emails</td>
            <td>Your email address and your message</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in responding to you (Art. 6(1)(f)); we delete the correspondence once the matter is closed, and within 12 months at the latest</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Data that stays on your device (section 1) and traffic to destinations you choose (section 2) are not
      processed by us at all. You are not required to provide any personal data. If you decline consent you
      see only limited ads. We make no decisions based solely on automated processing that have legal or
      similarly significant effects on you; with your consent, Google may use ad data to personalise the ads
      you see.
    </p>

    <h3>International transfers</h3>
    <p>
      Google may process data in the United States and other countries, relying on the EU-U.S. Data Privacy
      Framework, its UK Extension and the Swiss-U.S. Data Privacy Framework, and on Standard Contractual
      Clauses where needed (see
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy/frameworks" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google's data transfer frameworks</a>).
      We are based outside the EEA, but we receive none of the data described in section 3.
    </p>

    <h3>Your rights under the GDPR</h3>
    <p>
      You have the right to access, rectify and erase your personal data, to restrict or object to its
      processing, to data portability, and to withdraw consent at any time without affecting the lawfulness
      of processing before the withdrawal. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data
      protection supervisory authority, in particular in the country where you live or work; in the UK this
      is the Information Commissioner's Office, and in Switzerland the Federal Data Protection and
      Information Commissioner. Section 13 explains how to exercise these rights.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, you may have rights to access, correct, delete, or port your personal
      data, or to object to certain processing. Since PingDeck stores your data only on your device, you can
      exercise these rights directly in the app. For anything related to advertising data held by Google, use
      the Privacy options screen in the app and Google's own controls. If you need help, write to
      <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a> and we will respond within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the updated version is published on this page with a new "updated" date.
      Material changes affecting how data is shared will also be noted in the app's release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>15. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Yaner Apps &mdash; <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
